ntabs) and negatively (a third test account was denied with the expected cronie error). Test artifacts (testuser, temp crontabs) were fully cleaned up, leaving only the intended configuration, confirmed by a final check. The executor additionally ran the required independent node-restart evidence protocol: a pre-restart healthy baseline, a backgrounded reboot with immediate acknowledgement, a directly observed boot-id change after the connection dropped and recovered, and confirmation that crond, cron.allow, and opsadmin's cron access all survived the reboot. Hygiene is clean; the only residue is a harmless /tmp/reboot.log from the shutdown command.","limitations":["Persistence/reboot-survival was not itself part of the public task contract, so while the executor's restart evidence is strong and well-formed, it is treated as supplemental confidence-building rather than a scored material outcome.","No independent confirmation that /etc/cron.allow (rather than some other mechanism) is what RHEL9's cronie build actually consults was captured beyond the observed allow/deny behavior itself; however, the observed denial message is consistent with expected cronie allow-list semantics."]},"ttft_ms":25147,"type":"result","duration_ms":92461,"uuid":"f3d818bf-9562-41dd-b137-db96b0e76b1d","ttft_stream_ms":918,"time_to_request_ms":42,"first_content_frame_ms":1409,"queued_turn_count":0}
